|~ The verbs of any value [D62], and the contracts of the verbs that
   several kinds answer, each residing on `::qlang/any`, the kind beneath
   every kind. A contract is a verb without a body: its page and its
   examples are the laws of every verb of its name, what a residence
   leaves out it says, and a call whose subject no residence serves
   reaches it and is refused with `:addresses`, the residences of the
   name [D67], [D72]. ~|

:gt
  |~~ Whether the subject follows the value the call gives in the one
      order of their kind. Numbers, strings, keywords and tag names each
      compare with a value of their own kind, a residence on each of
      them checking both [D65]; a subject of another kind reaches this
      contract.

      ~(10 | gt 5 | eq true)
      ~("b" | gt "a" | eq true)
      ~(null | gt 1 !| /addresses | has ::number/gt)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:gte
  |~~ Whether the subject follows or equals the value the call gives in
      the one order of their kind, which `gt` states for every kind it
      compares.

      ~(10 | gte 10 | eq true)
      ~(:b | gte :a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:lt
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes the value the call gives in the one
      order of their kind, which `gt` states for every kind it compares.

      ~(5 | lt 10 | eq true)
      ~("a" | lt "b" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:lte
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes or equals the value the call gives in
      the one order of their kind, which `gt` states for every kind it
      compares.

      ~(10 | lte 10 | eq true)
      ~(::a | lte ::b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:count
  |~~ The number of elements a container holds: a vector's, a set's,
      and a map's entries.

      ~([1 2 3] | count | eq 3)
      ~(#[:a :b] | count | eq 2)
      ~({:x 1 :y 2 :z 3} | count | eq 3)
      ~(42 | count !| /addresses | has ::vec/count)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:empty
  |~~ Whether a container holds no element.

      ~([] | empty | eq true)
      ~([1] | empty | eq false)
      ~({} | empty | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:first
  |~~ The first element of a vector, the least of a set, or the first
      value of a map, and null for an empty container.

      ~([10 20 30] | first | eq 10)
      ~(#[:c :a :b] | first | eq :a)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | first | eq 1)
      ~([] | first | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:last
  |~~ The last element of a vector, the greatest of a set, or the last
      value of a map, and null for an empty container.

      ~([10 20 30] | last | eq 30)
      ~(#[:c :a :b] | last | eq :c)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | last | eq 2)
      ~([] | last | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:sum
  |~~ The sum of the elements of a vector or a set, or of the values of a
      map, each a number; an empty container sums to 0.

      ~([1 2 3 4] | sum | eq 10)
      ~([] | sum | eq 0)
      ~(#[1 2 3] | sum | eq 6)
      ~({:a 10 :b 20} | sum | eq 30)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::SumElementNotNumberError
  |~~ An element `sum` adds must be a number; `:index` names it.
      ~(
        [1 "two" 3] | sum
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::SumElementNotNumberError ::string 1]
       )
  ~~|

::SumResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ Every element is of the right kind, and the running
      total still answered outside the finite-double domain. The
      total is read at each element in the subject's order, so a subject
      whose partial sums leave the domain lifts here while its
      mathematical total sits inside it — `:index` names the element
      the total crossed at, and the subject splits there.

      ~(
        [1e308 1e308] | sum
        !| [type /index]
        |  eq [::SumResultNotFiniteError 1]
       )
  ~~|

:min
  |~~ The first element in the one order of values, the order `sort`
      answers, of a vector, a set, or the values of a map; an empty
      container yields null.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | min | eq 1)
      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | min | eq 1)
      ~([3 "a" null] | min | eq null)
      ~(#[3 1 4] | min | eq 1)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:max
  |~~ The last element in the one order of values, the order `sort`
      answers, of a vector, a set, or the values of a map; an empty
      container yields null.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | max | eq 5)
      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | max | eq 3)
      ~([3 "a" null] | max | eq "a")
      ~(#[3 1 4] | max | eq 4)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:filter
  |~~ Keeps the elements of a container whose predicate answers true,
      in a container of the same kind; the predicate runs against each
      element as its subject, a map's value among them, and answers a
      boolean. A verb runs against each element as a quote does.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq [3 4 5])
      ~([{:age 25} {:age 15}] | filter ~(/age | gte 18) | eq [{:age 25}])
      ~([1 -2 3] | :positive ::verb~(gt 0) | filter ~positive | eq [1 3])
      ~(#[1 -2 3] | filter ::verb~(gt 0) | eq #[1 3])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq {:b 2 :c 3})
      ~({} | filter ~(gt 0) | eq {})
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::FilterConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
      ~(
        [1 2] | filter ~(1)
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::FilterConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::FilterPredicateNotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `filter ~(gt 1)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | filter (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::FilterPredicateNotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|

:every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every element of a
      container, a map's elements being its values; it stops at the
      first false, and an empty container answers true.

      ~([2 4 6 8] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      ~([1 2 3] | every ~(gt 2) | eq false)
      ~([2 4 6] | :positive ::verb~(gt 0) | every ~positive | eq true)
      ~([] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      ~({:a 1 :b -2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | eq false)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | every ::verb~(gt 0) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::EveryConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
      ~(
        [1 2] | every ~(1)
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::EveryConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::EveryPredicateNotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `every ~(gt 0)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | every (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::EveryPredicateNotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|

:any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some element of a
      container, a map's elements being its values; it stops at the
      first true, and an empty container answers false.

      ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2) | eq true)
      ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 99) | eq false)
      ~([1 2 3] | :big ::verb~(gt 2) | any ~big | eq true)
      ~([] | any ~(gt 0) | eq false)
      ~({:a -1 :b 0 :c 2} | any ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      ~({:a -1 :b 2} | any ::verb~(gt 0) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::AnyConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for each element;
      `:index` names the element whose predicate answered another kind.
      ~(
        [1 2] | any ~(1)
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::AnyConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::AnyPredicateNotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `any ~(gt 2)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | any (count | gt 1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AnyPredicateNotQuoteError ::boolean]
       )
  ~~|

:groupBy
  |~~ Partitions a vector or a set into a map keyed by the keyword the key
      answers for each element: a vector's buckets are vectors and a
      set's are sets. The entries follow the first occurrence of each
      key, and each bucket the subject's order.

      ~([{:dept :eng :name "a"} {:dept :sales :name "b"} {:dept :eng :name "c"}] | groupBy ~(/dept) | /eng * /name | eq ["a" "c"])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::GroupByKeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ `groupBy ~(/key)` — the key sub-pipeline must produce a Keyword for every element; one element produced a non-keyword.
      ~(
        [{:k 1} {:k 2}] | groupBy ~(/k)
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::GroupByKeyNotKeywordError ::number 0]
       )
  ~~|

::GroupByKeyNotQuoteError
  |~~ The key of `groupBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `groupBy ~(/kind)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [{:kind :a}] | groupBy :a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::GroupByKeyNotQuoteError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|

:indexBy
  |~~ Collapses a vector or a set into a map keyed by the keyword the key
      answers for each element; of two elements the key answers alike,
      the last wins.

      ~([{:id :a :name "alice"} {:id :b :name "bob"}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a/name | eq "alice")
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::IndexByKeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ `indexBy ~(/key)` — the key sub-pipeline must produce a Keyword for every element; one element produced a non-keyword.
      ~(
        [{:k 1}] | indexBy ~(/k)
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::IndexByKeyNotKeywordError ::number 0]
       )
  ~~|

::IndexByKeyNotQuoteError
  |~~ The key of `indexBy` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `indexBy ~(/id)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [{:id :a}] | indexBy :a
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IndexByKeyNotQuoteError ::keyword]
       )
  ~~|

:sort
  |~~ The elements in the one order of values: by kind first, null,
      boolean, number, string, keyword, tag name, vector, set, map,
      quote, doc, error and elision, then every other tag by its name;
      within a kind numbers by value, strings by their code units,
      keywords and tag names by their names, vectors element by element,
      a set as its vector, maps by their keys and then their values, and
      a tagged value by its payload. A vector and a set sort into a
      vector, since the order is imposed and a set keeps its own; a map
      sorts its entries by their values and keeps the keys. `sort ~(key)`
      orders by the value the key answers for each element, a vector
      serving as a compound key, and keeps elements with equal keys in
      the subject's order; the descending order is the sort reversed.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | sort | eq [1 1 3 4 5])
      ~([3 null "x" 1] | sort | eq [null 1 3 "x"])
      ~(#[:y :x :z] | sort | eq [:x :y :z])
      ~([::B ::A] | sort | eq [::A ::B])
      ~([{:a 1 :b 2} {:a 1 :b 1}] | sort ~([/a /b]) | eq [{:a 1 :b 1} {:a 1 :b 2}])
      ~([3 null 1] | sort ~([(eq null) /]) | eq [1 3 null])
      ~([{:k 1} {:k 2}] | sort ~(/k) | reverse | eq [{:k 2} {:k 1}])
      ~({:a 3 :b 1 :c 2} | sort | vals | eq [1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::SortKeyNotQuoteError
  |~~ The key of `sort` is code it runs on each element, so it takes a
      quote, `sort ~(/age)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [3 1 2] | sort 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SortKeyNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:take
  |~~ The first count elements in the subject's order, a set's least
      among them, and a map's first count entries, in a container of the
      subject's kind. The count is a whole number: past the length the
      whole container comes back, and a negative count takes nothing.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | take 3 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(#[:d :c :b :a] | take 2 | eq #[:a :b])
      ~([1 2 3] | take -1 | eq [])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::TakeCountNotIntegerError
  |~~ The count of `take` must be a whole number — a fractional count
      cannot name a prefix length.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | take 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::TakeCountNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:drop
  |~~ The container with its first count elements removed, a map's first
      count entries, in a container of the subject's kind. The count is
      a whole number: past the length the empty container comes back,
      and a negative count drops nothing.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | eq [3 4 5])
      ~(#[:d :c :b :a] | drop 2 | eq #[:c :d])
      ~([1 2 3] | drop -1 | eq [1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::DropCountNotIntegerError
  |~~ The count of `drop` must be a whole number — a fractional count
      cannot name how many to remove.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | drop 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DropCountNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:at
  |~~ Indexed access: the element of a vector or a set at an integer
      index, a set indexing in its one order and a negative index
      counting from the end, or the value of a map at a key; a miss
      answers null, the soft counterpart of the strict `/key`
      projection.

      ~([10 20 30] | at 1 | eq 20)
      ~([10 20 30] | at 99 | eq null)
      ~(#[:a :b :c] | at -1 | eq :c)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | at :a | eq 1)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::AtIndexNotIntegerError
  |~~ The index of `at` on a vector or a set must be a whole number.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | at 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AtIndexNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:distinct
  |~~ The constructor of the set: the elements of a vector in the one
      order of values, each once [D16], so a downstream verb receives a
      value whose kind says «no duplicates»; a set passes through as it
      is. Duplication is decided by structural equality, the axiom of
      `eq`, so two maps, vectors or sets of the same content collapse;
      `#[…]` and `::set[…]` mint the same value.

      ~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
      ~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | payload | eq [1 2 3])
      ~([{:id 1} {:id 2} {:id 1}] | distinct | eq #[{:id 1} {:id 2}])
      ~(#[1 2 3] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:reverse
  |~~ The container in reverse order: a vector and a set reverse into a
      vector, since a set keeps its own order, and a map reverses its
      entries and keeps the keys.

      ~([1 2 3] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
      ~(#[:a :b :c] | reverse | eq [:c :b :a])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | vals | eq [2 1])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:flat
  |~~ One level of nesting flattened: the vectors and sets among the
      elements are spliced in, the other elements pass through, and the
      answer is of the subject's kind, so a set of sets flattens into
      their union.

      ~([[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat | eq [1 2 3 4 5])
      ~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | flat | eq #[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:reduce
  |~~ The universal left-fold: threads an accumulator across the
      elements of a vector, a set in its order, or the values of a map,
      and collapses them to one value. The seed is the first accumulator,
      returned as it is for an empty subject; the reducer is applied as
      `reducer accumulator element` — an operand of two places folded
      through its bound form (`acc | add element`), or a verb with the
      accumulator as its subject and the element in its first slot.
      `sum`, `count`, `max` and the builders of structures all factor
      through it.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 15)
      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 1 ~(mul) | eq 120)
      ~(["a" "b" "c"] | reduce "" ~(append) | eq "abc")
      ~([#[1] #[2 3]] | reduce #[] ~(union) | eq #[1 2 3])
      ~(:max2 ::verb~(:x ::number | if (gt x) ~(/) ~(x)) | [3 1 4 1 5] | reduce 0 ~max2 | eq 5)
      ~(:tally ::verb~(:_ ::any | add 1) | [10 20 30] | reduce 0 ~tally | eq 3)
      ~([] | reduce 42 ~(add) | eq 42)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

::ReduceReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ `reduce` reducer must be a binary operand or a verb whose first
      slot takes the element. An inline expression, a literal or an
      unbound name carries no (accumulator, element) binding.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(42)
        !| type
        |  eq ::ReduceReducerNotBinaryError
       )
  ~~|

::ReduceReducerNotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer of `reduce` is code it runs on the accumulator with each element, so it takes a
      quote, `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a value computed at the call is refused.
      ~(
        [1 2 3] | reduce 0 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ReduceReducerNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:union
  |~~ The union of two operands of one kind: two sets merged in the one
      order, or two maps merged with the second one's value winning a
      key both hold; over a vector of operands it folds them. The bound
      form `a | union b` reads b against a, and the full form `union a
      b` reads both against the value in the pipe.

      ~(#[:a :b] | union #[:b :c] | eq #[:a :b :c])
      ~({:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~([#[:a :b] #[:b :c]] | union | eq #[:a :b :c])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:minus
  |~~ The first operand without what the second holds: a set without the
      elements of another, a map without the keys of a map, a vector or a
      set of keywords; over a vector of operands it folds them.

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | minus #[:b] | eq #[:a :c])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b]] | minus | eq #[:a :c])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:inter
  |~~ What the first operand and the second both hold: the elements two
      sets share, or the entries of a map whose keys a map, a vector or a
      set of keywords holds; over a vector of operands it folds them.

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | inter #[:b :d] | eq #[:b])
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b :d]] | inter | eq #[:b])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:has
  |~~ Whether a map holds a key or a set holds an element.

      ~({:name "Alice"} | has :name | eq true)
      ~(#[:a :b :c] | has :b | eq true)
      ~(42 | has :k !| /addresses | has ::map/has)
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:prepend
  |~~ The subject with the operand in front: a string takes a string as
      its prefix, a vector any value as its first element. The subject
      keeps its kind and the tags over it, a set staying a set through
      its constructor.

      ~("world" | prepend "hello " | eq "hello world")
      ~([2 3] | prepend 1 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(42 | prepend "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::string/prepend ::vec/prepend])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:append
  |~~ The subject with the operand after it: a string takes a string as
      its suffix, a vector any value as its last element. The subject
      keeps its kind and the tags over it, a set staying a set through
      its constructor.

      ~("hello" | append " world" | eq "hello world")
      ~([1 2] | append 3 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(42 | append "x" !| /addresses | eq #[::string/append ::vec/append])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:eq
  |~~ Whether the subject equals the value, by structural equality deep
      across maps, vectors, sets and scalars.

      ~(42 | eq 42 | eq true)
      ~({:a 1} | eq {:a 1} | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:value ::any | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/eq})

:type
  |~~ The value's kind, a tag: the tag of a tagged value, an error, a
      verb, a binding record, a descriptor of the catalog, a quote and
      a set among them, and for every other value the kind of the core
      its literal implies (`::number`, `::string`, `::keyword`,
      `::tag`, `::vec`, `::map`, …), named under the prefix `qlang/`
      and written short [D32]. `type | docs` reads the kind's page, and
      asking what a value is composes `type` with `eq`, `filter ~(type
      | eq ::string)`.

      `result !| type` names the tag of an error: the descriptor `!|`
      materializes carries it as every tagged value carries its tag.

      ~(42 | type | eq ::number)
      ~("hi" | type | eq ::string)
      ~(:foo | type | eq ::keyword)
      ~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)
      ~(#[1 2] | type | eq ::set)
      ~(::verb~(42) | type | eq ::verb)
      ~(
        "hello" | add 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AddLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::tag | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/type})

:json
  |~~ The subject rendered as a JSON string. The shapes of qlang alone
      lose their identity at this boundary: a keyword becomes its bare
      name, a key and a value alike (`:foo` → `"foo"`), a set flattens
      to an array, and a map writes its keys as the keys of an object.
      It hands values to consumers that do not read qlang.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | json | eq "{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}")
      ~([:foo :bar] | json | eq "[\"foo\",\"bar\"]")
      ~(#[1 2 3] | json | eq "[1,2,3]")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/json})

:if
  |~~ The branch the condition chooses, run against the subject: `if
      cond ~(then) ~(else)`. The condition is a boolean computed at the
      call, each branch a quote, and only the chosen branch runs.

      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")
      ~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")
      ~({:age 25} | if (/age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")
      ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq 105)

      A condition that is not a boolean is refused, since it has no
      second reading [D14]. The empty quote `~()` is the branch that
      leaves the subject as it is. ~~|
  ::verb~(:condition ::boolean | :then ::quote | :else ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/if})

::IfConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The condition of `if` answers a boolean; null, a number, a string
      or any other value is refused where it stands.
      ~(
        5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no")
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfConditionNotBooleanError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::IfThenNotQuoteError
  |~~ The then branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true "yes" ~("no")
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfThenNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::IfElseNotQuoteError
  |~~ The else branch of `if` is code it runs only when chosen, so it
      takes a quote, `if (gt 0) ~("yes") ~("no")`; every branch is checked at the call.
      ~(
        5 | if true ~("yes") "no"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::IfElseNotQuoteError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:coalesce
  |~~ The first answer of the alternatives, run in order against the
      subject, that is neither null nor an error; every other value,
      false, 0, "", [], {} and #[] among them, ends the walk, and a walk
      that skips them all answers null. `coalesce ~(/key) ~(fallback)`
      reads "the key where the map holds it, else the fallback": `/key`
      answers an error on a map without the key, which the walk skips as
      it skips null.

      ~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "alice")
      ~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) | eq "ace")
      ~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce ~(/missing) ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")
      ~({:nickname null :name null} | coalesce ~(/nickname) ~(/name) ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(* :alternatives ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/coalesce})

::CoalesceNoAlternativesError
  |~~ `coalesce` requires at least one captured alternative sub-pipeline.
      ~(
        {} | coalesce
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CoalesceNoAlternativesError]
       )
  ~~|

:cond
  |~~ The branch of the first predicate that answers true against the
      subject, the clauses alternating predicate and branch, each
      predicate answering a boolean. A clause left over after the pairs
      is the default branch; without one, a walk that matches nothing
      answers null. It flattens a chain of nested `if`.

      ~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")
      ~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")
      ~({:role "admin"} | cond ~(/role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(/role | eq "user") ~("limited") | eq "full")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(* :clauses ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/cond})

::CondNoBranchesError
  |~~ `cond` requires at least one (predicate, branch) pair plus an optional trailing default.
      ~(
        5 | cond
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::CondNoBranchesError]
       )
  ~~|

::CondConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ Every predicate of `cond` answers a boolean; `:index` counts the
      modifiers from 1 and names the predicate that answered another
      kind.
      ~(
        5 | cond ~(1) ~("yes")
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::CondConditionNotBooleanError 1 ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:keyword
  |~~ The involution of strings and keywords: a string names its
      keyword, and a keyword answers its name, so `keyword | keyword`
      round-trips.

      ~("foo" | keyword | eq :foo)
      ~(:foo | keyword | eq "foo")
      ~(42 | keyword !| /addresses | eq #[::keyword/keyword ::string/keyword])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:parse
  |~~ The involution of source text and quotes, the way `keyword` flips
      a string and a keyword: text reads as the quote of its steps, and
      a quote prints back as its text.

      ~("add 1 2" | parse | eq ~(add 1 2))
      ~(~(add 1) | parse | eq "add 1")
      ~(42 | parse !| /addresses | eq #[::quote/parse ::string/parse])
  ~~|
  ::verb~()

:apply
  |~~ The value of the code run against the subject, under the fork
      rule: the declarations the code makes stay inside it, and only its
      value comes out. The subject comes first, as for every verb, so
      `x | apply /` runs a quote held as the subject against itself, and
      `"src" | parse | apply /` evaluates source text.

      The quote's first step rides `|` against the subject unless the
      quote carries a leading combinator (`*`, `!|`), which routes it
      through that combinator, so the pipeline suffix a `:trail` records
      runs again as it is.

      ~(42 | apply ~(type | eq ::null) | eq false)
      ~([1 2 3] | apply ~(* mul 2) | eq [2 4 6])
      ~([[1 2] [3 4]] | apply ~(flat | sort) | eq [1 2 3 4])
      ~("10 | add 3" | parse | apply / | eq 13)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:code ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/apply})

::ApplyCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ `apply` requires its captured arg to answer a Quote — the
      code it runs against the subject.

      ~(
        42 | apply 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ApplyCodeNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
